  [OK] D1a the Newtonian solve is validated against a CLOSED-FORM field, not against another solve: for an exponential sphere with M(<r) analytic the mesh returns g_N to a worst 1.19% over 5-80 kpc. This is the pipeline the MI law is applied to, so its accuracy is the accuracy of every MI number below

  In SPHERICAL symmetry the AQUAL field equation integrates to mu(x) x = y exactly, so MI and AQUAL are the
  SAME THEORY there and the last column above is EXACTLY 1.000000 in the continuum. Whatever it is not is the
  numerical floor of this lane's central comparison:
      MI/AQUAL = 0.99167 mean, spread 0.00403 across 5-80 kpc
      => floor = 0.95% in acceleration = 0.37 sigma on v_c(R0) and 0.12 sigma on Sigma_dyn.
  Any MI-vs-AQUAL difference below that is numerics, not theory.
  [OK] D1b the floor is a bounded, radius-INDEPENDENT offset -- 0.95% at worst with only 0.40% of spread over 5-80 kpc -- i.e. a systematic of the two pipelines and not noise. It is measured where MI and AQUAL are provably IDENTICAL theories, so it is the right yardstick for the disc difference: 0.37 sigma on v_c, 0.12 sigma on Sigma_dyn

  Milgrom 2022 (PRD 106, 064060) builds MI at the level of the EQUATION OF MOTION and notes that such theories
  "are not necessarily governed by an action"; what they DO require is that x mu(x) be monotonic so the law
  inverts. Verified for Route A over eighteen decades:
      x mu(x) strictly increasing: True       y nu(y) strictly increasing: True
      closed-form derivative identity holds to 1.93e-09;  min of h(s) = 2e^s - 2 - s is 1.00e-06
  [OK] D1c ROUTE A SATISFIES MILGROM 2022's REQUIREMENT, verified not assumed: x mu(x) and y nu(y) are both strictly increasing over eighteen decades, and the closed form d/ds[s^2/(1-e^-s)] = s e^-s (2e^s - 2 - s)/(1-e^-s)^2 reproduces a finite-difference derivative to 1.9e-09 with h(s) = 2e^s - 2 - s > 0. So the exponential kernel IS an admissible MI kernel in Milgrom's sense -- which is precisely the sense that does not come with an action

  the Newtonian y = |g_bar|/a0 that MI reads at (f_M, f_R) = (1.78, 0.80): 1.5715 in the plane, 1.5255 at |z| = 1.1 kpc
      -> nu = 1.3995 and 1.4100;  u = sqrt(y) = 1.254 and 1.235, decades below the u ~ 37 float64 saturation
  [OK] D1d both Milky Way observables sit in the kernel's transition region ON THE NEWTONIAN FIELD (y = 1.57 radial, 1.53 vertical), with the vertical point at the LOWER y -- so MI's two boosts differ between the two evaluation points even though they are identical at any single point, and nothing here hides in a saturated limb of the kernel

==============================================================================================================
  D2  WHAT THE MI ESCAPE COSTS -- measured, in energy units, not asserted
==============================================================================================================
  The corpus's 2026-08-01 result stands: for this form class the MI law is NOT VARIATIONAL in a disc
  (three no-goes -- not variational; the u-contraction (v/c)^2-suppressed for generic K; the prefactor IS the
  worldline's Frenet torsion). Here is that cost as a NUMBER rather than a citation. A conservative field has
  zero circulation. The algebraic law a = nu(|g_N|/a0) g_N has curl nu'(|g_N|) (grad|g_N| x g_N), which
  vanishes only when the level surfaces of |g_N| coincide with those of Phi_N -- true in spherical symmetry,
  FALSE in a disc. Measured on an ANALYTIC Miyamoto-Nagai disc (M = 5.5e10 Msun, a = 2.5 kpc, b = 0.3 kpc) so
  the Newtonian control is exact to machine precision and none of this is mesh error:

  loop (R kpc) x (z kpc)       work on one leg   Newtonian circ.      rel    MI circulation      /leg   /(vc^2/2)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  (7-9) x (0.0-0.5)                 9.6919e+09        -1.252e-06  1.3e-16        1.0900e+08  +0.01125   +4.01e-03
  (6-10) x (0.0-1.1)                1.9763e+10        -1.669e-06  8.4e-17        2.9335e+08  +0.01484   +1.08e-02
  (4-12) x (0.0-2.0)                4.2775e+10         5.722e-06  1.3e-16        7.9582e+08  +0.01860   +2.93e-02
  (2-20) x (0.0-4.0)                8.3882e+10         0.000e+00  0.0e+00        1.9210e+09  +0.02290   +7.07e-02
  [OK] D2a *** THE MI FIELD IS NOT CONSERVATIVE IN A DISC, and this is the price of the escape. *** The Newtonian control closes to 1.3e-16 of one leg's work (machine precision -- it IS a gradient), while the algebraic MI field leaves a residual circulation of up to 2.29% of a leg: a star gains up to 7.1% of the Galaxy's specific circular kinetic energy per closed circuit in the (R, z) plane. No potential, so no energy integral -- and the vertical Jeans analysis that DEFINES the 73.9 target assumes both. AQUAL has this identically zero by construction

   |z| [kpc]   AQUAL boost on g_R   AQUAL boost on g_z   vert/rad   MI nu (both)  MI vert/rad
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
       0.010              1.39647              1.42084    1.01745        1.39953      1.00000
       0.250              1.39850              1.41286    1.01027        1.39656      1.00000
       0.500              1.40297              1.41060    1.00543        1.39740      1.00000
       1.100              1.41859              1.42067    1.00147        1.41004      1.00000
       2.000              1.44970              1.44979    1.00006        1.44039      1.00000

  POINTWISE, AQUAL's boost is anisotropic and MI's is exactly isotropic -- but the anisotropy is LARGEST in the
  midplane (1.0175), where g_z ~ 0 and it cannot matter, and SMALLEST at |z| = 1.1 kpc (1.0015),
  which is where the constraint is actually read. Across the two evaluation points the FIT uses -- v_c at
  (R0, 0) and Sigma_dyn at (R0, 1.1 kpc) -- the ratio is AQUAL 1.0173 against MI 1.0075, a
  0.97% difference. That is the entire size of the escape this lane is testing,
  and D1b's numerical floor is 0.95%.
  [OK] D2b the structural difference is REAL but small exactly where it is needed: AQUAL's vertical/radial boost anisotropy falls monotonically from 1.0175 in the midplane to 1.0015 at |z| = 1.1 kpc and 1.0001 at 2 kpc, while MI's is exactly 1 at every point by construction. Over the two points the five-constraint fit reads, AQUAL gives 1.0173 against MI's 1.0075, so MI predicts a ~1.0% SMALLER vertical force at the same rotation curve -- the same size as the 0.95% numerical floor

